CertHub.register({"id":"az-802","vendor":"Microsoft","name":"Microsoft Certified: Windows Server Administrator Associate (exam AZ-802: Administering Windows Server)","short":"AZ-802","exam":"AZ-802","blurb":"One exam for Windows Server administrators working on-premises, in Azure and hybrid: AD DS and hybrid identity, Windows Admin Center, Azure Arc and migration, Hyper-V and Azure VMs, DNS/DHCP and hybrid networking, storage and failover clustering, security hardening, and monitoring, backup and troubleshooting.","status":"check","statusNote":"Skills measured as reported from the Microsoft Learn AZ-802 study guide on Sept 24, 2026: deploy and manage AD DS 20–25%; manage Windows Server instances and workloads in a hybrid environment 10–15%; manage virtual machines 10–15%; on-premises and hybrid networking 10–15%; storage and file services 15–20%; secure Windows Server infrastructure 10–15%; monitor and troubleshoot Windows Server environments 15–20%. Midpoints (22.5/12.5/12.5/12.5/17.5/12.5/17.5 = 107.5) were scaled to 100 and rounded by largest remainder to 21/11/12/12/16/12/16; the four 10–15% areas tie, so which one gets 11 is arbitrary. learn.microsoft.com and most training sites were blocked by the network proxy, so the ranges came from search excerpts of the official study guide and training sites, not the page itself. Search results also say the AZ-801 content (failover clustering, Storage Spaces Direct, Hyper-V Replica and Azure Site Recovery, backup, migration with Storage Migration Service, Azure Migrate and ADMT) is folded into these seven areas, but the exact placement could not be confirmed; this plan puts clustering under storage, replication and Site Recovery under virtual machines, migration under hybrid management and AD DS, and backup and AD recovery under monitoring and troubleshooting. Re-check the study guide, question count and exam length before booking.","lastVerified":"2026-09-24","notices":[{"from":"2026-09-24","until":"2027-03-31","text":"Exams AZ-800 and AZ-801 retired on Sept 30, 2026 at 5:00 PM Central time. AZ-802 is now the single exam for Microsoft Certified: Windows Server Administrator Associate (formerly Windows Server Hybrid Administrator Associate). Existing holders keep the credential and renew against the new skills. If you passed only one of AZ-800 or AZ-801 before the cutoff, you need AZ-802 for the certification."}],"examInfo":{"questions":"Not published (Microsoft associate exams are usually 40–60 questions, some case studies)","minutes":100,"pass":"700 on a 1–1000 scale","extra":"AZ-802 replaces the two-exam path (AZ-800 + AZ-801) with one exam covering both. Seat time is about 120 minutes including agreements and survey; confirm the exam length and any lab items on the AZ-802 exam page."},"examSim":{"questions":50,"minutes":100},"sources":[{"label":"Microsoft Learn: Exam AZ-802 page","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/credentials/certifications/exams/az-802/"},{"label":"Microsoft Learn: AZ-802 study guide (skills measured)","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/credentials/certifications/resources/study-guides/az-802"},{"label":"Microsoft Learn: Windows Server Administrator Associate certification","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/credentials/certifications/windows-server-administrator-associate/"}],"planWeeks":10,"hoursPerWeek":"8–10","domains":[{"id":1,"name":"Deploy and manage AD DS","w":21,"topics":["Deploying domain controllers: Install-ADDSForest / Install-ADDSDomainController, install from media (IFM), read-only DCs, DCs on Azure VMs (static private IP on the NIC, NTDS on a data disk with host caching off)","FSMO roles (schema master, domain naming master, RID master, PDC emulator, infrastructure master): placement, transfer vs seize","Sites, subnets, site links, link cost and bridging; replication health with repadmin and dcdiag","Forest, external, shortcut and realm trusts; transitivity and direction; selective authentication; SID filtering","Users, groups (domain local, global, universal), OUs and delegation of control; group managed service accounts and the KDS root key","Default Domain Policy vs fine-grained password policies (PSOs); AD Recycle Bin","Hybrid identity: Entra Connect Sync (including staging mode) vs Entra Cloud Sync; password hash sync, pass-through authentication, seamless SSO","Group Policy processing (LSDOU), Enforced and Block Inheritance, security filtering, loopback processing, Central Store, backup and restore","Migrating AD objects between domains and forests with ADMT and SID history; domain and forest functional levels when upgrading DCs"],"notes":["Skills measured: Deploy and manage AD DS (20–25%)"],"labs":["Windows Server 2025 evaluation (free, 180 days) in Hyper-V or VirtualBox: build a two-DC forest, add a second site with its own subnet and site link, force replication with `repadmin /syncall /AdeP` and read `repadmin /replsummary`. Transfer the PDC emulator to DC2 and back with `Move-ADDirectoryServerOperationMasterRole`.","Enable the AD Recycle Bin, create a fine-grained password policy for an 'Admins-PSO' group, confirm it with `Get-ADUserResultantPasswordPolicy`, then create a gMSA (after `Add-KdsRootKey`) and install it on a member server with `Install-ADServiceAccount`.","Free Azure account (Entra ID Free tenant): install the Entra Cloud Sync provisioning agent on a lab member server, scope it to one OU and confirm test users appear in Entra ID. Cost warning: the agent is free, but do not leave any Azure VM running; delete the configuration and uninstall the agent when done."]},{"id":2,"name":"Manage Windows Server instances and workloads in a hybrid environment","w":11,"topics":["Windows Admin Center: desktop vs gateway mode, extensions, Kerberos constrained delegation, Windows Admin Center for Azure VMs and Arc-enabled servers","PowerShell remoting: Enter-PSSession vs Invoke-Command, Just Enough Administration (JEA) role capability and session configuration files","Azure Arc-enabled servers: Connected Machine agent (azcmagent), at-scale onboarding with a service principal, extensions, tags and RBAC","Azure Policy and machine configuration for Arc-enabled and Azure servers; audit vs deploy effects","Azure Update Manager: assessments, one-time updates, maintenance configurations; hotpatching for Windows Server","Azure Automation runbooks and hybrid runbook workers","Storage Migration Service: inventory, transfer and cut over of file servers, including identity takeover","Azure Migrate: discovery and assessment, server migration of Hyper-V, VMware and physical servers to Azure","Upgrading and migrating server roles (in-place upgrade paths, migrating DHCP, print and IIS workloads)"],"notes":["Skills measured: Manage Windows Server instances and workloads in a hybrid environment (10–15%); includes migration content carried over from AZ-801 (placement not confirmed)"],"labs":["Windows Server evaluation VM: install Windows Admin Center, add two lab servers, then create a JEA endpoint that lets a 'HelpDesk' group only run Restart-Service for the Spooler service and test it with `Enter-PSSession -ConfigurationName`.","Two evaluation servers: fill a share on SRV1 with test files, then use the Storage Migration Service in Windows Admin Center to inventory SRV1, transfer the data and shares to SRV2 and run cutover so SRV2 takes over SRV1's name and IP. Confirm the old UNC path still works.","Free Azure account: onboard one on-premises evaluation VM to Azure Arc with the generated script, check `azcmagent show`, and assign a built-in machine configuration baseline policy in audit mode. Cost warning: Arc onboarding is free, but machine configuration on Arc servers, Update Manager, Defender for Servers and Log Analytics ingestion are billed; set a budget alert and remove the Arc resource and policy the same day."]},{"id":3,"name":"Manage virtual machines","w":12,"topics":["Hyper-V VM configuration: generation 1 vs 2, dynamic memory, integration services, enhanced session mode, Secure Boot and virtual TPM","Nested virtualization requirements; PowerShell Direct","Virtual disks: VHD vs VHDX, fixed, dynamic and differencing disks; shared VHDX / VHD Set","Checkpoints: production vs standard; why checkpoints are not backups","Hyper-V virtual switches (external, internal, private) and Switch Embedded Teaming","Live migration and storage migration between Hyper-V hosts; Kerberos vs CredSSP authentication","Hyper-V Replica: primary and replica servers, replication frequency, recovery points, planned and unplanned failover, test failover","Azure Site Recovery for Hyper-V and Azure VMs: recovery plans, test failover, RPO and failback","Azure VMs running Windows Server: Azure Hybrid Benefit, Sysprep and Azure Compute Gallery, extensions, Azure Edition hotpatching"],"notes":["Skills measured: Manage virtual machines (10–15%); includes Hyper-V Replica and Azure Site Recovery carried over from AZ-801 (placement not confirmed)"],"labs":["On a Windows Server evaluation host with Hyper-V, create a Gen 2 VM from a differencing disk based on a sysprepped parent VHDX, take a production checkpoint, then use PowerShell Direct (`Invoke-Command -VMName`) to rename it.","With two evaluation Hyper-V hosts (nested VMs are fine: `Set-VMProcessor -ExposeVirtualizationExtensions $true`), enable Hyper-V Replica on a small VM with a 5-minute frequency, run a test failover, then a planned failover and reverse replication.","Free Azure account: deploy a small Windows Server VM (B-series, no public IP), connect with Run Command, add the Custom Script Extension to install IIS. Read the Azure Site Recovery tutorial instead of enabling it. Cost warning: VMs, disks and Site Recovery protected instances are billed; delete the whole resource group when finished and set a budget alert first."]},{"id":4,"name":"Implement and manage an on-premises and hybrid networking infrastructure","w":12,"topics":["DNS zones: primary, secondary, stub and AD-integrated; replication scope; secure dynamic updates","Forwarders, conditional forwarders and root hints; DNS policies and zone scopes","DNSSEC signing, trust anchors and the Name Resolution Policy Table (NRPT)","Azure DNS private zones, virtual network links and auto-registration; Azure DNS Private Resolver","DHCP scopes, reservations, options and relay; authorization in AD","DHCP high availability: failover in load balance and hot standby modes; IPAM","Azure VNet addressing and static private IPs set on the NIC","Hybrid connectivity: site-to-site and point-to-site VPN, ExpressRoute, Azure Network Adapter"],"notes":["Skills measured: Implement and manage an on-premises and hybrid networking infrastructure (10–15%)"],"labs":["On your evaluation DC, create a conditional forwarder, a stub zone, and a DNS policy that returns a different A record to one client subnet (`Add-DnsServerClientSubnet`, `Add-DnsServerQueryResolutionPolicy`). Test with `Resolve-DnsName`.","On a second evaluation server, install DHCP, authorize it, create a scope with exclusions and a reservation, then configure failover in hot standby mode with the first server and check `Get-DhcpServerv4Failover`.","Free Azure account: create a VNet with a private DNS zone linked with auto-registration; deploy a tiny test VM and confirm its record appears. Cost warning: the private zone costs cents a month, but a VPN gateway or DNS Private Resolver costs over a dollar a day, so read about them instead of deploying, and delete the resource group afterward."]},{"id":5,"name":"Manage storage and file services","w":16,"topics":["Azure File Sync: sync groups, cloud and server endpoints, cloud tiering policies","Azure Files with AD DS authentication; share-level RBAC vs NTFS permissions","SMB security: encryption, signing, SMB over QUIC, removing SMBv1","File Server Resource Manager quotas and file screens; DFS Namespaces and DFS Replication","Storage Spaces resiliency and provisioning; ReFS vs NTFS; Data Deduplication","Failover clustering: validation, cluster networks, quorum models and witnesses (disk, file share, cloud), Cluster-Aware Updating","Storage Spaces Direct: minimum nodes, cache, resiliency; Scale-Out File Server for application data","Storage Replica: synchronous vs asynchronous, server-to-server and stretch cluster; guest clustering with shared VHDX"],"notes":["Skills measured: Manage storage and file services (15–20%); includes failover clustering and Storage Spaces Direct carried over from AZ-801 (placement not confirmed)"],"labs":["Windows Server evaluation VM with four extra virtual disks: build a mirrored thin-provisioned ReFS space, remove one disk and watch the pool report degraded while data stays online; then add an FSRM hard quota and an active file screen on a share.","Three nested evaluation VMs: run `Test-Cluster`, create a two-node failover cluster with a file share witness on the third VM, add a clustered file server role and fail it over with `Move-ClusterGroup`. Then enable Cluster-Aware Updating in self-updating mode.","Free Azure account: create a Standard storage account, a small file share and a Storage Sync Service, register an evaluation server and add a server endpoint with cloud tiering. Cost warning: capacity and transactions cost cents, but each extra registered server is billed monthly; also note a cloud witness needs only a storage account. Unregister the server and delete the resource group when done."]},{"id":6,"name":"Secure Windows Server infrastructure","w":12,"topics":["Security baselines: OSConfig on Windows Server 2025, Microsoft Security Compliance Toolkit baselines, drift control","Credential Guard and virtualization-based security; LSA protection","App Control for Business (WDAC) policies and audit mode; AppLocker differences","Windows LAPS: backing up local admin passwords to AD DS or Entra ID, rotation and retrieval permissions","Hardening domain controllers: tiered administration, Protected Users, privileged access workstations, restricting who can log on to DCs","Windows Defender Firewall profiles, rules and connection security (IPsec) rules","Microsoft Defender for Servers via Defender for Cloud: onboarding Arc and Azure servers, recommendations, just-in-time VM access","Encryption: BitLocker on servers and Azure VM disk encryption options; SMB signing and encryption"],"notes":["Skills measured: Secure Windows Server infrastructure (10–15%)"],"labs":["On a Windows Server 2025 evaluation member server, apply the OSConfig security baseline (`Set-OSConfigDesiredConfiguration -Scenario SecurityBaseline/WS2025/MemberServer -Default`), then check compliance with `Get-OSConfigDesiredConfiguration`. Note which settings changed.","Enable Windows LAPS with Group Policy to back up the local admin password to AD DS, update the schema with `Update-LapsADSchema`, grant the help desk read rights with `Set-LapsADReadPasswordPermission`, and retrieve it with `Get-LapsADPassword`.","Create an App Control for Business policy in audit mode from the default Windows template, deploy it to a test server, run an unsigned tool and read the CodeIntegrity event log to see what would have been blocked. Only enforce after reviewing events, and keep a VM checkpoint so you can roll back."]},{"id":7,"name":"Monitor and troubleshoot Windows Server environments","w":16,"topics":["Performance Monitor counters and data collector sets; baselines; Resource Monitor","Event logs, custom views and event subscriptions (Windows Event Forwarding)","Windows Admin Center alerts and System Insights predictive capacity","Azure Monitor agent, data collection rules, VM insights and Log Analytics queries for hybrid servers","Troubleshooting connectivity and name resolution (Test-NetConnection, Resolve-DnsName, ipconfig /flushdns)","Windows Update, time service (w32tm) and Kerberos troubleshooting; Arc agent and extension troubleshooting (azcmagent check)","Azure VM troubleshooting: boot diagnostics, Serial Console, Run Command, redeploy","AD DS recovery: Directory Services Restore Mode, authoritative vs non-authoritative restore, authoritative SYSVOL (DFSR) restore","Backup: Windows Server Backup, bare-metal and system state backup, Azure Backup with the MARS agent and MABS"],"notes":["Skills measured: Monitor and troubleshoot Windows Server environments (15–20%); includes backup content carried over from AZ-801 (placement not confirmed)"],"labs":["On an evaluation server, build a user-defined data collector set for CPU, memory, disk queue and network counters, run it during a load test (for example `diskspd` or copying large files), and read the report to name the bottleneck.","Set up Windows Event Forwarding: configure a collector-initiated subscription on DC1 that gathers Security event 4625 (failed logons) from a member server, generate failures and confirm they arrive under Forwarded Events.","Take a system state backup of a lab DC with `wbadmin start systemstatebackup`, delete a test OU, boot into DSRM, restore and mark the OU authoritative with `ntdsutil`. Optional free Azure account: protect a folder with the MARS agent to a Recovery Services vault. Cost warning: Azure Backup charges per protected instance plus storage; stop protection and delete backup data and the vault afterward."]}],"study":{"1":[["When do you transfer a FSMO role and when do you seize it?","Transfer when the current holder is online. Seize only when the holder is permanently lost; afterwards that DC must never come back and needs metadata cleanup."],["How do fine-grained password policies apply, and what decides the winner?","A PSO applies to users or global security groups, not OUs. The lowest precedence number wins, and a PSO linked directly to the user beats one linked through a group."],["Compare password hash sync and pass-through authentication.","PHS validates in the cloud, keeps working if on-premises is down and enables leaked-credential detection. PTA validates against on-premises DCs through agents, so sign-in depends on them."],["What does SID history do during an ADMT migration?","It copies the old domain SID into the migrated account's sIDHistory so the user keeps access to resources still ACLed with the old SID until permissions are re-ACLed. SID filtering on the trust must allow it."],["What is the Group Policy processing order, and what do Enforced and Block Inheritance do?","Local, site, domain, then OUs parent to child; the last applied wins. Block Inheritance stops higher GPOs, but an Enforced link above still applies and wins."]],"2":[["What is JEA and why use it?","A constrained PowerShell endpoint: a role capability file lists allowed cmdlets and parameters, and a session configuration maps groups to roles, often running as a virtual account. Operators do their task without admin rights."],["What does Azure Arc give an on-premises Windows Server?","An Azure resource identity so you can use Azure Policy and machine configuration, Update Manager, Azure Monitor, Defender for Cloud, tags and RBAC without moving the workload."],["Describe the three phases of Storage Migration Service.","Inventory reads shares, data and configuration from the source; Transfer copies data and shares to the destination; Cut over moves the source's name and IP to the destination so clients keep using the same path."],["When would you use Azure Migrate instead of Storage Migration Service?","Azure Migrate discovers, assesses and replicates whole servers (Hyper-V, VMware, physical) to Azure VMs. Storage Migration Service moves file server data, shares and identity to another server."],["How do you onboard many servers to Arc without interactive sign-in?","Create a service principal with the Azure Connected Machine Onboarding role and deploy the at-scale script with Group Policy, Configuration Manager or another tool."]],"3":[["Compare generation 1 and generation 2 Hyper-V VMs.","Gen 2 uses UEFI with Secure Boot, supports vTPM and boots from SCSI or the network. Gen 1 uses BIOS and IDE boot for older guests. You cannot convert in place."],["Explain Hyper-V Replica planned vs unplanned failover.","Planned failover is run when the primary is healthy: shut down the primary VM, send the last changes, then fail over with no data loss and reverse replication. Unplanned failover is started on the replica when the primary is lost, recovering to the latest (or a chosen) recovery point with possible data loss."],["What does an Azure Site Recovery recovery plan add?","It groups machines to fail over together in a set order, with scripts or manual steps between groups, so multi-tier apps come up correctly. You test it with a test failover into an isolated network."],["Why are checkpoints not backups?","A checkpoint is a differencing chain stored with the VM, so storage failure loses both; long chains also hurt performance. Use them briefly before changes and delete them afterward."],["What is needed for live migration without shared storage?","Hosts in the same domain (or cluster), matching processor vendor (or processor compatibility mode), live migration enabled with Kerberos (plus constrained delegation) or CredSSP, and a network for migration traffic. Shared-nothing live migration moves storage and memory together."]],"4":[["Forwarder, conditional forwarder, stub zone: when do you use each?","A forwarder sends all unresolved queries upstream. A conditional forwarder sends one domain's queries to named servers. A stub zone keeps another zone's NS records current automatically."],["How do Azure VMs resolve on-premises names and vice versa?","Azure DNS Private Resolver: the outbound endpoint and a forwarding ruleset send on-premises domain queries to on-premises DNS; the inbound endpoint gives on-premises forwarders an IP for Azure private zones."],["Load balance vs hot standby DHCP failover?","Load balance has both servers active and splitting clients. Hot standby keeps one active and the partner holding a reserve of addresses, which suits a central standby for branches."],["Why set a static private IP in Azure rather than inside the guest?","Azure DHCP always gives the NIC its configured IP; a different address set in Windows cuts the VM off. Make it static on the NIC and leave the guest on DHCP."],["Site-to-site VPN vs point-to-site vs ExpressRoute?","S2S links a whole network over IPsec; P2S connects individual machines; ExpressRoute is a private provider circuit with predictable latency that does not cross the internet."]],"5":[["Why does a failover cluster need a witness, and which one fits a two-node cluster across two sites?","The witness gives an extra vote so the cluster keeps quorum when half the nodes are lost. A cloud witness (an Azure storage account) is ideal when there is no third site."],["What does Cluster-Aware Updating do?","It patches cluster nodes one at a time: drains roles off a node, installs updates, restarts it, brings roles back and moves to the next, so the clustered service stays online."],["Storage Replica synchronous vs asynchronous?","Synchronous writes to both sites before acknowledging, so no data loss but it needs low latency (short distance). Asynchronous acknowledges locally first, tolerating distance at the cost of possible data loss."],["How does Azure File Sync cloud tiering decide what to tier?","The volume free space policy keeps a percentage of the volume free by tiering the coldest files; the optional date policy tiers files not accessed within N days. The free space policy always wins."],["Scale-Out File Server vs a general-purpose clustered file server?","SOFS is active-active for application data such as Hyper-V VHDX and SQL files on CSVs. A general-purpose file server is active-passive for user shares with many small files."]],"6":[["What does Credential Guard protect, and what does it need?","It isolates NTLM hashes and Kerberos TGTs in a virtualization-based security container so tools that dump LSASS cannot read them. It needs 64-bit, UEFI with Secure Boot and virtualization extensions; do not enable it on domain controllers."],["Why start App Control for Business in audit mode?","Audit mode logs what would be blocked without blocking it, so you can find legitimate apps missing from the policy before enforcement stops business software."],["What does Windows LAPS solve?","It gives every machine a unique, rotated local administrator password stored in AD DS or Entra ID with access control, stopping lateral movement with a shared local admin password."],["Name four ways to harden domain controllers.","Only Tier 0 admins log on to DCs, admins use privileged access workstations and Protected Users, no other roles or browsing on DCs, apply the DC security baseline, keep them patched and restrict RDP and management ports."],["What is just-in-time VM access?","A Defender for Servers feature that keeps management ports closed in the NSG or Azure Firewall and opens them only on approved request, for a limited time and source IP."]],"7":[["What is a data collector set and why build a baseline?","A saved group of performance counters, traces and configuration data collected on a schedule. A baseline of normal values lets you spot bottlenecks and capacity trends later."],["Authoritative vs non-authoritative AD restore?","Non-authoritative restores a DC from backup and then lets replication update it. Authoritative marks restored objects with higher version numbers so they replicate out and overwrite deletions on other DCs, used to recover deleted objects when the Recycle Bin is not enabled."],["How do you do an authoritative SYSVOL restore with DFSR?","On the chosen good DC set msDFSR-Options=1 and disable its SYSVOL subscription, disable the others, force AD replication, then re-enable the authoritative DC first and the rest afterward so they sync from it."],["Kerberos authentication fails on one server with clock errors. What do you check?","Time skew over five minutes breaks Kerberos. Check `w32tm /query /status` and the source; domain members sync from DCs and the forest root PDC emulator should sync from a reliable external source."],["Windows Server Backup vs Azure Backup MARS agent?","Windows Server Backup is local: bare-metal, system state, volumes to disk. The MARS agent backs up files, folders and system state directly to a Recovery Services vault in Azure for offsite retention; MABS adds workload and VM backups."]]},"qCount":88,"hasLessons":true,"hasLessonsEs":true,"hasPbqs":true});
